{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":27781,"name":"Jordan Mack","username":"jm9k","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/talk.nervos.org/jm9k/{size}/3197_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-24T01:50:17.589Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eCKBHash is a term you might see within the various CKB libraries, and for basic usage it’s abstracted away for you. For more advanced lock design you need to understand what it is and where it is commonly applied. We put together a quick guide to explain how these conventions work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch1\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eUsing the CKBHash Function in CKB Development\u003c/h1\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen building smart contracts on CKB you are free to use any cryptographic function that you wish to use. However, many of the system scripts and within the commonly used libraries rely on Blake2b.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this post we describe some of the specific CKB conventions on how to use it properly with the existing foundations. In most cases, you will rely on libraries that handle all of these details for you. However, advanced smart contract designs might benefit from understanding the specifics of how it is commonly used on CKB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-the-ckb-blake2b-standard-ckbhash-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-the-ckb-blake2b-standard-ckbhash-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eThe CKB Blake2b Standard (ckbhash)\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWithin the libraries you will find a function called “\u003cstrong\u003eckbhash\u003c/strong\u003e”. This is the a specific configuration of Blake2b:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAlgorithm\u003c/strong\u003e: blake2b-256 (outputs 32 bytes).\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePersonalization\u003c/strong\u003e: “ckb-default-hash”.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCommon Variant\u003c/strong\u003e: blake160 (blake2b-256 truncated to first 20 bytes (160 bits)).\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBlake2b-256 is always used for the ckbhash function. However, you will see some places that reference “blake160”. This is still blake2b-256, but the hash hash been truncated to the first 20 byptes (160 bits).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/c/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c.png\" data-download-href=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c\" title=\"Ckbhash always outputs 32 bytes. Blake160 keeps the first 20 bytes and drops the last 12. Example: the ckbhash of a public key is c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7c82c5c82651aa688cf6dee26. The first 20 bytes, c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7, become the lock arg.\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/c/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c_2_690x313.png\" alt=\"Ckbhash always outputs 32 bytes. Blake160 keeps the first 20 bytes and drops the last 12. Example: the ckbhash of a public key is c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7c82c5c82651aa688cf6dee26. The first 20 bytes, c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7, become the lock arg.\" data-base62-sha1=\"tai1baQqy7QxjBTIrip1hisFtne\" width=\"690\" height=\"313\" srcset=\"https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/c/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c_2_690x313.png, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/optimized/2X/c/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c_2_1035x469.png 1.5x, https://talk.nervos.org/uploads/default/original/2X/c/cc68d69c228825db58ea81cb9f080b0135122f0c.png 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"DFECF4\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003eCkbhash always outputs 32 bytes. Blake160 keeps the first 20 bytes and drops the last 12. Example: the ckbhash of a public key is c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7c82c5c82651aa688cf6dee26. The first 20 bytes, c8328aabcd9b9e8e64fbc566c4385c3bdeb219d7, become the lock arg.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1040×472 37.7 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe ckbhash function automatically applies the “ckb-default-hash” personalization, so you never need to manually configure the blake2b parameters when using library functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-common-uses-in-ckb-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-common-uses-in-ckb-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCommon Uses in CKB\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe ckbhash function is used throughout CKB for:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSecp256k1 Lock Arg (Blake160)\u003c/strong\u003e - The Secp256k1 lock requires a ckbhash of the public key truncated to the first 20 bytes.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eScript Hash Calculation\u003c/strong\u003e - Hashing serialized script structures for identification.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransaction Hashing\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating transaction identifiers (excluding witnesses).\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransaction Witness Hash\u003c/strong\u003e - Hashing the complete serialized transaction including witnesses for block headers.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCell Data Hashing\u003c/strong\u003e - Generating data hashes for type scripts and cell references.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSigning Message Construction\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating the message to sign by hashing tx hash plus witness data.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-1-public-key-to-secp256k1-lock-arg-blake160-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-1-public-key-to-secp256k1-lock-arg-blake160-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e1. Public Key to Secp256k1 Lock Arg (Blake160)\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eConverts an secp256k1 public key into the required arg for the commonly used secp256k1_blake160_sighash_all lock, which is used in most wallets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { SignerCkbPrivateKey, ccc } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst signer = new SignerCkbPrivateKey(new ccc.ClientPublicTestnet(), privateKey);\nconst lockArg = (await signer.getAddressObjSecp256k1()).script.args;  // Internally: ckbhash(publicKey).slice(0, 20)\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-2-script-hash-calculation-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-2-script-hash-calculation-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e2. Script Hash Calculation\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGenerates a unique identifier for any script by hashing its serialized structure, which is used for script identification and locating cells.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { Script } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst script = new Script(\n\t\"0x9bd7e06f3ecf4be0f2fcd2188b23f1b9fcc88e5d4b65a8637b17723bbda3cce8\",\t// codeHash\n\t\"type\",\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t// hashType\n\t\"0xb39bbc0b3673c7d36450bc14cfcdad2d559c6c64\"\t\t\t\t\t\t\t// args\n);\n\nconst scriptHash = script.hash();  // Internally: ckbhash(molecule_encode(script))\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-3-transaction-hashing-6\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-3-transaction-hashing-6\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e3. Transaction Hashing\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCreates a unique identifier for transactions by hashing the raw transaction data, which excludes witnesses and is used for transaction references.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { Transaction } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst tx = new Transaction(/* ... */);\nconst txHash = tx.hash();  // Internally: ckbhash(molecule_encode(raw_transaction))\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-4-transaction-witness-hash-7\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-4-transaction-witness-hash-7\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e4. Transaction Witness Hash\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComputes the complete transaction hash including all witness data, which is used in block headers for the witness merkle tree.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { Transaction } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst tx = new Transaction(/* ... */);\nconst witnessHash = tx.hashFull();  // Internally: ckbhash(molecule_encode(full_transaction))\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-5-cell-data-hashing-8\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-5-cell-data-hashing-8\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e5. Cell Data Hashing\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGenerates a hash of cell data content, which is used by type scripts for data integrity verification and locating cells.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { HasherCkb } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst hasher = new HasherCkb();\nhasher.update(cellData);\nconst dataHash = hasher.digest();  // Internally: ckbhash(cellData)\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-h-6-signing-message-construction-9\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-h-6-signing-message-construction-9\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003e6. Signing Message Construction\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBuilds the final message to be signed by combining transaction hash with witness data, which ensures signatures cover the complete transaction context.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { Signer } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\nconst signature = await signer.signTransaction(tx);  // Internally: sign(ckbhash(txHash + witnessData))\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-complete-example-with-ccc-10\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-complete-example-with-ccc-10\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eComplete Example with CCC\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis example demonstrates the complete workflow from private key to script hash, showing how ckbhash is used at multiple stages: first to generate the blake160 lock arg from a public key, then to create a unique script identifier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"javascript\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-javascript\"\u003eimport { Script, SignerCkbPrivateKey, ccc } from \"@ckb-ccc/core\";\n\n// Create signer from private key\nconst client = new ccc.ClientPublicTestnet();\nconst signer = new SignerCkbPrivateKey(client, \"0xd00c06bfd800d27397002dca6fb0993d5ba6399b4238b2f29ee9deb97593d2bc\");\n\n// Get blake160 lock arg\nconst lockArg = (await signer.getAddressObjSecp256k1()).script.args;\n\n// Create lock script\nconst lockScript = new Script(\n\t\"0x9bd7e06f3ecf4be0f2fcd2188b23f1b9fcc88e5d4b65a8637b17723bbda3cce8\",  // mainnet secp256k1\n\t\"type\",\n\tlockArg\n);\n\n// Get script hash\nconst scriptHash = lockScript.hash();\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-27781-further-reading-11\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-27781-further-reading-11\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eFurther Reading\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.nervos.org/docs/how-tos/how-to-sign-a-tx\"\u003eHow to Sign a Transaction\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Detailed explanation of transaction signing process.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.nervos.org/docs/sdk-and-devtool/ccc\"\u003eCCC Documentation\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Official CCC usage guide.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/ckb-system-scripts\"\u003eCKB System Scripts Repository\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Source code for all CKB system scripts.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/rfcs/blob/master/rfcs/0022-transaction-structure/0022-transaction-structure.md\"\u003eCKB RFCs - Transaction Structure (RFC 0022)\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Defines the official ckbhash function specification.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/rfcs/blob/master/rfcs/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list.md\"\u003eCKB RFCs - Genesis Script List (RFC 0024)\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Lists all system scripts including secp256k1_blake160_sighash_all.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis post was originally published on the Sōnami blog: \u003ca href=\"https://blog.sonami.cc/posts/using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development/\" class=\"inline-onebox\"\u003eUsing the CKBHash Function in CKB Development | Sōnami Blog\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":3,"updated_at":"2026-11-24T01:50:17.589Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":3,"reads":34,"readers_count":33,"score":61.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":11362,"topic_slug":"using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development","display_username":"Jordan Mack","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://docs.nervos.org/docs/sdk-and-devtool/ccc","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"JavaScript/TypeScript (CCC) | Nervos CKB","clicks":0},{"url":"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/rfcs/blob/master/rfcs/0022-transaction-structure/0022-transaction-structure.md","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"rfcs/rfcs/0022-transaction-structure/0022-transaction-structure.md at master · nervosnetwork/rfcs · GitHub","clicks":0},{"url":"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/rfcs/blob/master/rfcs/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list.md","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"rfcs/rfcs/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list/0024-ckb-genesis-script-list.md at master · nervosnetwork/rfcs · GitHub","clicks":0},{"url":"https://blog.sonami.cc/posts/using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Using the CKBHash Function in CKB Development | Sōnami Blog","clicks":0},{"url":"https://github.com/nervosnetwork/ckb-system-scripts","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - nervosnetwork/ckb-system-scripts: CKB's official system scripts. Those contracts will be included in system cells in genesis blocks · GitHub","clicks":0},{"url":"https://docs.nervos.org/docs/how-tos/how-to-sign-a-tx","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"How to Sign a Transaction | Nervos CKB","clicks":0},{"url":"https://talk.nervos.org/t/ckb-ecosystem-biweekly-update/9821/35","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"CKB Ecosystem Biweekly Update","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":3}],"moderator":false,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":1731,"hidden":false,"trust_level":4,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development/11362/1","babel_translations_meta":[{"language":"zh-cn","status":"completed","source_language":"en"},{"language":"es","status":"completed","source_language":"en"}],"show_translation_widget":true,"show_translation_button":true,"babel_detected_locale":"en","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":3,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false,"can_translate":false},{"id":27799,"name":"LCL","username":"LusoCryptoLabs","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/talk.nervos.org/lusocryptolabs/{size}/11500_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-24T09:19:33.294Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eOne trap worth adding: blake160 has to be the 32 byte ckbhash cut to 20 bytes. A blake2b hasher built with a 20 byte output (\u003ccode\u003eBlake2bBuilder::new(20)\u003c/code\u003e) gives a different value, because the digest length is part of blake2b’s parameters. A lock arg made that way never matches, and cells sent to it cannot be unlocked.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWe keep this as a lesson on CKB Pulse, taken from a real fix: \u003ca href=\"https://pulse.lusocryptolabs.com/learned/scripts/ckb-blake160-is-the-first-20-bytes-of-the-32-byte-ckbhash\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eCKB blake160 is the first 20 bytes of the 32 byte ckbhash · CKB Pulse\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":3,"updated_at":"2026-11-24T09:19:33.294Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":24,"readers_count":23,"score":4.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":11362,"topic_slug":"using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development","display_username":"LCL","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://pulse.lusocryptolabs.com/learned/scripts/ckb-blake160-is-the-first-20-bytes-of-the-32-byte-ckbhash","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"CKB blake160 is the first 20 bytes of the 32 byte ckbhash · CKB Pulse","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":4356,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/using-the-ckbhash-function-in-ckb-development/11362/2","babel_translations_meta":[{"language":"es","status":"completed","source_language":"en"},{"language":"zh-cn","status":"completed","source_language":"en"}],"show_translation_widget":true,"show_translation_button":true,"babel_detected_locale":"en","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_translate":false},{"id":27818,"name":"Joii2020","username":"joii2020","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/talk.nervos.org/joii2020/{size}/10348_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-25T05:01:40.252Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eOne potential pitfall to watch out for: if you are working with a third-party project, verify whether its \u003ccode\u003eblake2b-256\u003c/code\u003e initialization actually uses \u003ccode\u003eckb-default-hash\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":3,"updated_at":"2026-11-25T05:01:40.252Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":17,"readers_count":16,"score":3.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":11362,"topic_slug":"using